# Trivy ignore list for the runtime BASE image scan in .github/workflows/security.yml.
#
# Same rule as scripts/audit-gate.mjs's allowlist: an entry needs a written reason
# and an upgrade path, and it is deleted the moment upstream ships a fix. This list
# is the triage record, not a mute button. Anything not listed here still fails CI.
#
# What this gate scans is `node:22-alpine` — an image we consume and do not build.
# We cannot patch it; we can only pin a newer tag when one exists, or stop shipping
# the affected component. Both are noted per entry.

# openssl DoS via unbounded memory growth. Alpine has published 3.5.8-r0; NO published
# node base tag carries it yet — measured 2026-08-26 by reading the package out of each
# image rather than assuming: node:20-alpine 3.5.6-r0, node:22-alpine 3.5.7-r0,
# node:24-alpine 3.5.7-r0. So there is no tag to pin our way out of this one today.
# Same reachability argument as the entry above: nothing in these containers terminates
# TLS — Caddy does, in its own image. Delete this line once any base we can move to ships
# 3.5.8-r0 or later.
CVE-2026-14456

# Language packages are NOT scanned here (--pkg-types os). Everything trivy reported
# in that class lives in npm's OWN bundle inside the base image
# (/usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm) — brace-expansion, cross-spawn, glob, tar. That is
# not our dependency tree: ours is gated by scripts/audit-gate.mjs against the lockfile,
# which is the thing we can actually fix.
#
# Nor is any of it shipped by something that runs: the images execute `node dist/main.js`,
# healthchecks are busybox wget, migrations run host-side. npm is present only because
# the base image includes it. Deleting it from the runtime stage is the real fix and
# belongs with the image work — until then, scanning a vendor's bundled copies on every
# PR produces a permanently red gate nobody can turn green, which is how a gate stops
# being read.
